    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de01ee5e_8df0682e",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 5614,
      "author": {
        "id": 1158184
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-29T19:58:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thinking about it again, rather than creating a host command specifically to check that the seed has been set, maybe we could make it a bit more generic to query the status of the \"FP encryption engine\". We could return, say a uint32_t and various bits would have status information. bit0 would fp_seed_is_set, in the future bit1 could be entropy_is_set or something similar, etc.\nDoes that make sense?\nIn that case we\u0027d probably want the first field of that struct to be a version number so we know the format of that structure. Or maybe even a separate command that queries the version of the \"FP encryption engine\", rather than having a \"version\" field in every structure. Wdyt?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5614,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 5614,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "8308cc8ed35a5c944f95cbde74a5184cf6d380e3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ebb36373_d733e8a1",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 5614,
      "author": {
        "id": 1345639
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-30T01:03:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree that making the struct generic would be nice. I think since we are using a struct, putting various information as fields in the struct would be more readable. The benefit of using various bits of a uint32_t is that information is more compact (seed_is_set now only needs one bit instead of a uint8_t).\n\nBut since the bit definition macros would live in this header as well, like FP_MODE_ENROLL_IMAGE, we still need to change ec_commands.h when we want to use a new bit. \n\nI don\u0027t want to introduce a separate command that just queries the version - this functionality seems small for a command. But I am not sure.\n\nI am going with bits in a uint32_t and a version number now.",
      "parentUuid": "de01ee5e_8df0682e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5614,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 5614,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "8308cc8ed35a5c944f95cbde74a5184cf6d380e3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
